
 You are an expert iOS developer using Swift and SwiftUI. Follow these guidelines:


  # Product / MLP Vision (Mindset Ritual App)
  - **What we're building:** Gratitude + mindset journaling meets Duolingo — gamified, habit-forming, fun.
  - **Psychology basis:** Positive Psychology, Stoic, CBT, evidence-based daily exercises.
  - **Curated prompts (quiz-driven):** Gratitude, memento mori, goal setting, what went well, "what did you forget to give yourself credit for?", etc.
  - **Gamification:** Daily streak, XP, archetype building, weekly summaries, AI feedback, animations, haptics, sound effects. Progress that motivates.
  - **Future:** XP → Zen garden or visual progress representation on Dashboard. See `Context.md` for full MLP roadmap.


  # Swift & iOS Rules

  # Modularization Rules
  - Business logic belongs in `Domain`.
  - API and Persistence belong in `Data`.
  - Views belong in `Feature[Name]` modules.
  - Never import a Feature module into another Feature module (use the Coordinator).

  
  # AI Rules
  - Use `gemini-2.0-flash` for all AIService calls.
  - Ensure API keys are never hardcoded; suggest `Secrets.plist` or Environment Variables.

 
  # Scoping (for complex changes)
  - Prefer one package or one use case/flow per Composer request. For multi-file edits, name the target files or modules so changes stay predictable and reviewable.


  # Code Structure
  - Use Swift's latest features and protocol-oriented programming
  - Prefer value types (structs) over classes
  - Use MVVM architecture with SwiftUI
  - Structure: Features/, Core/, UI/, Resources/
  - Follow Apple's Human Interface Guidelines
  - **ViewModels stay clean:** ViewModels hold state and business logic only. Do not use HapticManager, UI types (Color, Font, View), or animation APIs in ViewModels—Views own haptics, animations, and visual feedback. ViewModels must not import SharedUtils for HapticManager; only Views call HapticManager when handling user actions or observing state.

  
  # Naming
  - camelCase for vars/funcs, PascalCase for types
  - Verbs for methods (fetchData)
  - Boolean: use is/has/should prefixes
  - Clear, descriptive names following Apple style
  - Data layer types that use the SwiftData framework use the `SD` prefix (e.g. `SDMindsetEntry`, `SDMindsetRepository`, `SDPersistenceService`). Domain and Feature modules do not use this prefix.
  - New screens/flows: add a Feature module, then register in `FeatureNavigation` (MainCoordinator) and app `AppViewFactory`; never import one Feature into another.
 
 
  # Swift Best Practices
  - Strong type system, proper optionals
  - async/await for concurrency
  - Result type for errors
  - @Published, @StateObject for state
  - Prefer let over var
  - Protocol extensions for shared code


  # Design System (SharedUI)
  - **Colors:** Always use `MindsetColors` from SharedUI. Never use raw `Color.orange`, `Color.green`, `.white`, etc. Use semantic tokens: `backgroundGrouped`, `textPrimaryAdaptive`, `accentOrange`, `successGreen`, `stoicSlateSoft`, `achievementGold`, etc.
  - **Fonts:** Always use `MindsetFonts` from SharedUI. Never use raw `.font(.headline)` or `.font(.caption)`. Use semantic tokens: `displayHeadline`, `promptHeadline`, `body`, `label`, `button`, etc.
  - **Layout:** Always use `MindsetLayout` from SharedUI for spacing, padding, corner radius, and dimensions. Never use magic numbers (e.g. `spacing: 12`, `.padding(25)`, `cornerRadius: 16`). Use tokens: `MindsetLayout.spacing12`, `MindsetLayout.paddingCard`, `MindsetLayout.radiusCard`, `MindsetLayout.buttonHeight`, etc.
  - Feature modules must add SharedUI as a dependency to use MindsetColors, MindsetFonts, and MindsetLayout.
  - **Modals:** Use `DismissButton(action: { ... })` from SharedUI to dismiss modals and full-screen flows (onboarding, ritual, etc.). Do not build custom close buttons for these; use the shared component for consistency.
  - **Haptics:** Use `HapticManager` (SharedUtils) only in Views—never in ViewModels. Call the semantic APIs (selection(), action(), success(), tick()) from View code when handling taps or when observing state (e.g. success when isAiThinking goes false).
  - See `Packages/SharedUI/Sources/SharedUI/MindsetColors.swift`, `MindsetFonts.swift`, and `MindsetLayout.swift` for full token lists. See `Context.md` §13 for usage summary.

  # UI Development
  - SwiftUI first, UIKit when needed
  - SF Symbols for icons
  - Support dark mode, dynamic type
  - SafeArea and GeometryReader for layout
  - Handle all screen sizes and orientations
  - Implement proper keyboard handling
  - Implement Apple's "Liquid Glass" for existing and new UI https://developer.apple.com/documentation/TechnologyOverviews/liquid-glass
  - Always use Swift 6 / Swift 2026 idioms.
  - Prefer `@Observable` over `ObservableObject`.
  - Use `Group` or `Section` in SwiftUI to avoid deeply nested view hierarchies.
  - When creating new Views, include:
    ```swift
    #if DEBUG
    @ObserveInjection var inject
    #endif
    // ... at end of body:
    .enableInjection()
    ```


  # Performance
  - Profile with Instruments
  - Lazy load views and images
  - Optimize network requests
  - Background task handling
  - Proper state management
  - Memory management


